<article class="env-lemma" id="08HX">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08HX">Lemma <span data-tag="08HX">15.62.6</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $A$, $B$ be $R$-algebras. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$A$ and $B$ are Tor independent over $R$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
for every pair of primes $\mathfrak p \subset A$ and $\mathfrak q \subset B$ lying over the same prime $\mathfrak r \subset R$ the rings $A_\mathfrak p$ and $B_\mathfrak q$ are Tor independent over $R_\mathfrak r$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For every prime $\mathfrak s$ of $A \otimes _ R B$ the module 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \text{Tor}_ i^ R(A, B)_\mathfrak s = \text{Tor}_ i^{R_\mathfrak r}(A_\mathfrak p, B_\mathfrak q)_\mathfrak s  \]
</div>
<p>
 (where $\mathfrak p = A \cap \mathfrak s$, $\mathfrak q = B \cap \mathfrak s$ and $\mathfrak r = R \cap \mathfrak s$) is zero. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\mathfrak s$ be a prime of $A \otimes _ R B$ as in (3). The equality 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \text{Tor}_ i^ R(A, B)_\mathfrak s = \text{Tor}_ i^{R_\mathfrak r}(A_\mathfrak p, B_\mathfrak q)_\mathfrak s  \]
</div>
    <p>
       where $\mathfrak p = A \cap \mathfrak s$, $\mathfrak q = B \cap \mathfrak s$ and $\mathfrak r = R \cap \mathfrak s$ follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/08HW" data-tag="08HW">15.62.3</a>. Hence (2) implies (3). Since we can test the vanishing of modules by localizing at primes (Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00HN" data-tag="00HN">10.23.1</a>) we conclude that (3) implies (1). For (1) $\Rightarrow $ (2) we use that 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \text{Tor}_ i^{R_\mathfrak r}(A_\mathfrak p, B_\mathfrak q) = \text{Tor}_ i^ R(A, B) \otimes _{(A \otimes _ R B)} (A_\mathfrak p \otimes _{R_{\mathfrak r}} B_\mathfrak q)  \]
</div>
    <p>
       again by Lemma <a href="/tag/08HW" data-tag="08HW">15.62.3</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>